Give the private bus of assert-bus no service directory

The fourth arm of assert-bus started its private bus from the standard
session configuration of the host. A bus of that form reads the
standard service directories. The probe of the daemon asks the portal
for the theme name, so the bus started the portal, and the portal
started its backends. The kill of the bus ended the portal, but a
backend that ignores the loss of the bus stayed as an orphan. The
build of the GTK theme runs the arm, so a build started that chain as
well.

The services of that chain ran with the home and the runtime directory
of the caller. The head comment of the script states that no run
reaches the files of the caller. The document portal of the chain also
stopped the live document portal when the runtime directory of the
caller reached the bus.

The arm now writes a bus configuration of its own and starts the bus
from that file. The file names no service directory, so no service
starts on demand and the probe connects and reads no name. The listen
address moves into the file, and the arm waits for the printed address
as before. The bus allows the EXTERNAL mechanism alone, so no cookie
keyring can appear in the home of the caller.

dbus-daemon logs one line for each start of a service. The arm now
reads the bus log after the daemon leaves and fails on such a line.
The exit codes, the success lines and the liveness reads of the four
arms stay as they were.

The change sits on the branch work/gtk4-theme of the local tree, as the
seventeenth commit. The fork wyn/qtcurve holds that branch. Merge request !15
on system/qtcurve holds that branch.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

diff --git a/gtk4/check/assert-bus.sh b/gtk4/check/assert-bus.sh
index 679c2fdd..f73ded89 100644
--- a/gtk4/check/assert-bus.sh
+++ b/gtk4/check/assert-bus.sh
@@ -186,12 +186,25 @@ echo "assert-bus: the watch mode with no --force lived past a first pass of" \
 
 # 4. The daemon leaves with the code 0 when the session bus dies, so a logout
 # stops a daemon that no unit holds. The arm needs dbus-daemon, and it names
-# the binary where the host holds none. The private bus holds no portal, so
-# the probe of the daemon connects and reads no name. That outcome is enough
-# for the handler of the lost bus.
+# the binary where the host holds none.
 if command -v dbus-daemon > /dev/null 2>&1; then
-    dbus-daemon --session --nofork --address="unix:abstract=$realname" \
-        --print-address > "$work/bus.log" 2>&1 &
+    # The configuration below names no service directory, so no service
+    # starts on demand and the probe of the daemon connects and reads no
+    # name. That outcome is enough for the handler of the lost bus.
+    cat > "$work/bus.conf" <<EOF
+<busconfig>
+  <type>session</type>
+  <listen>unix:abstract=$realname</listen>
+  <auth>EXTERNAL</auth>
+  <policy context="default">
+    <allow send_destination="*" eavesdrop="true"/>
+    <allow eavesdrop="true"/>
+    <allow own="*"/>
+  </policy>
+</busconfig>
+EOF
+    dbus-daemon --config-file="$work/bus.conf" --nofork --print-address \
+        > "$work/bus.log" 2>&1 &
     buspid=$!
     pids="$pids $buspid"
     wait_line 15 "$work/bus.log" "$realname" \
@@ -205,6 +218,13 @@ if command -v dbus-daemon > /dev/null 2>&1; then
     if [ "$code" -ne 0 ]; then
         fail "the daemon gave the code $code and not 0 after the bus died"
     fi
+    # dbus-daemon logs one "Activating service" line for each start of a
+    # service, and the private bus must start none.
+    starts=$(grep -cF "Activating service" "$work/bus.log" 2>/dev/null) ||
+        starts=0
+    if [ "$starts" -ne 0 ]; then
+        fail "the private bus started $starts services and not 0"
+    fi
     echo "assert-bus: the daemon left with the code 0 when the session bus" \
          "died"
 else
